---
/**
 * Engineering — the first half of the lead offer (ADR-0001).
 *
 * Written for a Researcher who owns a study and cannot analyse it because the
 * data layer underneath it does not exist yet. It names the four deliverables
 * ADR-0001 fixes, each as its own section, so the problem can be recognised by
 * scanning on a phone.
 *
 * No authorship question arises here — nobody is credited as the author of a
 * data pipeline — so the credit policy lives on the Analysis page alone
 * (ADR-0002). Nothing on this page may touch the faith stream or name the
 * owner's employer; `tests/services.test.ts` asserts both.
 */
import ContactCallToAction from '../../components/ContactCallToAction.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Engineering for clinical research — Dr. Wei-Hung Pan"
  description="Clinical data pipelines, reproducible analysis setups, apps and registry databases for clinical research groups. Fixed-scope packages, quoted per project."
>
  <h1>Your data is not ready for the analysis you need</h1>

  <p>
    The numbers exist. They are in four exports from the hospital system, two spreadsheets a
    research assistant maintained before they left, and a folder nobody has opened since the last
    grant report. Every analysis starts by rebuilding the dataset by hand, every rebuild comes out
    slightly different, and nobody can say which version produced the figure in the manuscript.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is an engineering problem, and it is the one I solve. I have worked with health and
    clinical data since 2012, and I build the data layer a study runs on — then run the statistics
    on it. A group can find a statistician in a university department and a developer on a
    freelance marketplace; one person who will build the data layer <em>and</em> defend the methods
    that come out of it is harder to find.
  </p>

  <h2>A clinical data pipeline</h2>

  <p>
    One documented path from the source export to an analysis-ready dataset: parsing, unit and
    code-system harmonisation, deduplication, derived variables, and validation rules that fail
    loudly instead of quietly dropping rows. It is a program, not an afternoon of cleaning, so the
    next extract takes minutes rather than another month.
  </p>

  <h2>A reproducible analysis setup</h2>

  <p>
    A pinned environment, version control, tests over the derived variables, and continuous
    integration that re-runs the whole thing on every change. A result a reviewer asks about two
    years from now can be regenerated from the raw export by a colleague who has never met you —
    which is increasingly what journals and funders mean when they ask for analysis code.
  </p>

  <h2>An app or dashboard</h2>

  <p>
    A small, purpose-built interface where a paper form and a spreadsheet are currently losing you
    data: structured entry with validation at the point of capture, screening and eligibility
    checks, and recruitment and completeness monitoring your coordinators can read without asking
    you first.
  </p>

  <h2>A registry or cohort database</h2>

  <p>
    A real schema instead of a widening spreadsheet: declared variables and permitted values,
    integrity across visits and across patients, access by role, an audit trail of who changed
    what, and an export that arrives in your analysis as a dataset rather than as another cleaning
    job. Built to outlive the person who set it up and the grant that paid for it.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is bought</h2>

  <p>
    Every engagement is a <strong>fixed-scope</strong> package, never an hourly rate.
    What you provide, what you receive and when it is finished are written down before
    any work starts. Billing by the hour against a messy dataset turns reliably into
    unbounded requests, which is bad for your budget and worse for your timeline.
  </p>

  <p>
    There are <strong>no published prices</strong>, because an honest one depends on
    how many sources feed the dataset, what state they are in, and what has to keep
    running after I hand it over. Tell me what you have and you get a written scope and
    a quote for the package — itemised for a grant office if your funding needs that.
  </p>

  <p>
    I work inside your institution's own infrastructure and governance. Identifiable
    patient data stays there: it does not come onto my machines, and a de-identified
    extract or a synthetic sample is enough to build against.
  </p>

  <h2>What I do not do</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Regulatory submission work.</strong> No submission-grade datasets or
      documentation feeding a filing to a regulator, and no system validation under
      that scope.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hospital IT.</strong> No server or network administration, no electronic
      health record configuration, no vendor platform procurement, no support rota.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hourly staff augmentation.</strong> I am not a pair of hands to be booked
      by the day against a backlog somebody else prioritises.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A system with nobody to hand it to.</strong> Every build ends in a
      handover to a named colleague of yours, with documentation they can work from. If
      there is no such person yet, say so at the start and we will scope that in rather
      than discover it at the end.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <ContactCallToAction
    locale="en"
    heading="Tell me what your data looks like"
    body="Send the study, the sources it comes from and the deadline you are working to. You get a written scope and a fixed price for the package — or an honest note that this is not work for me."
    action="Request a quote"
  />
</BaseLayout>
